<body>
<div class="wrap">

  <header>
    <div class="badge">Case Study · CRO</div>
    <h1>Auditing Notion's Landing Page with the LIFT Model</h1>
    <p class="byline">Santiago Batista Zelaya — Cognitive-emotional analysis applied to Conversion Rate Optimization</p>
  </header>

  <section>
    <h2>Context</h2>
    <p>I chose Notion's landing page (notion.so) as a practical exercise: a mature page, from a well-known brand, with years of CRO iteration behind it. The question that interested me wasn't "is it good or bad?" but something more precise: <strong>where does friction leak even on a landing page that's already very good?</strong></p>
    <p>To answer this I combined two layers of analysis: the <strong>LIFT Model</strong> (Value Proposition, Relevance, Clarity, Anxiety, Urgency) as a conversion framework, and a complementary read from cognitive psychology (Ekman, Zajonc, Theory of Mind). I ran the analysis with a tool I built myself, <a href="https://auditor-cognitivo.vercel.app" style="color:var(--accent)">Auditor Cognitivo</a>, designed to automate exactly this kind of diagnosis.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Page Structure (Attention Flow)</h2>
    <p class="sub">Attention capture by section, on a 0–10 scale</p>
    <div class="wire">
      <div class="wire-block"><div class="score">8</div><div class="name">Hero</div></div>
      <div class="wire-block"><div class="score">9</div><div class="name">Body (Use Cases)</div></div>
      <div class="wire-block"><div class="score">7</div><div class="name">Final CTA</div></div>
    </div>
    <blockquote>"Where teams and agents Think together" — Hero headline</blockquote>
    <p class="sub">Attention peaks in the body, not the hero — a sign that concrete use cases retain more attention than the opening value proposition, which still feels somewhat abstract.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>LIFT Score</h2>
    <p class="sub">Overall immediacy score: <strong>88/100</strong></p>
    <div class="bar-row"><div class="bar-label"><span>Value Proposition</span><span>9/10</span></div><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="width:90%"></div></div></div>
    <div class="bar-row"><div class="bar-label"><span>Relevance</span><span>8/10</span></div><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="width:80%"></div></div></div>
    <div class="bar-row"><div class="bar-label"><span>Clarity</span><span>7/10</span></div><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="width:70%"></div></div></div>
    <div class="bar-row"><div class="bar-label"><span>Anxiety Reduction</span><span>9/10</span></div><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="width:90%"></div></div></div>
    <div class="bar-row"><div class="bar-label"><span>Urgency</span><span>4/10</span></div><div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" style="width:40%"></div></div></div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Friction Diagnosis</h2>
    <div class="bullet"><span class="dot">◆</span><span>Clarity: the concept of "agents" in the hero assumes the visitor already distinguishes AI from autonomous agents — an abstraction that can lose users who still think in terms of traditional software.</span></div>
    <div class="bullet"><span class="dot">◆</span><span>Strength: Anxiety Reduction stands out — quantitative social proof ("100M users", "62% of Fortune 100") neutralizes corporate skepticism almost immediately.</span></div>
    <div class="bullet"><span class="dot">◆</span><span>Opportunity: Urgency is the weakest axis (4/10) — nothing in the hero pushes visitors to act now instead of continuing to evaluate other tools.</span></div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Complementary Cognitive Reading</h2>
    <div class="bullet"><span class="dot">◆</span><span><strong>Zajonc/Ekman:</strong> the clean copy generates pleasant cognitive fluency; promises like "Automate busywork" replace the frustration of daily information chaos with a sense of relief.</span></div>
    <div class="bullet"><span class="dot">◆</span><span><strong>Theory of Mind:</strong> the page accurately anticipates the mental state of a decision-maker tired of tech noise — phrases like "stay small while doing a lot" resonate with implicit budget fears.</span></div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Redesign Proposal (Hero A/B Test)</h2>
    <div class="grid2">
      <div class="opt">
        <h3>Option A — Functional Clarity</h3>
        <div class="uso">If users don't understand what AI agents actually do</div>
        <div class="copy"><strong>"The AI workspace that actually does the work for you."</strong><br>Centralize docs, wikis, and projects in one place where AI agents automate your busywork 24/7.</div>
        <div class="just">Replaces the abstraction with a concrete, verifiable benefit.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="opt">
        <h3>Option B — Urgency + Friction Removal</h3>
        <div class="uso">If the blocker is fear of hidden costs or technical integration</div>
        <div class="copy"><strong>"Scale your team's output. Not your overhead."</strong><br>Join 100M users. Write notes, find answers, and build agents with zero code.</div>
        <div class="just">Targets cost anxiety and adds competitive urgency backed by social proof.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Measurement Hypothesis (GA4)</h2>
    <p>If I had to prioritize one real test on this landing page, I'd measure:</p>
    <div class="bullet"><span class="dot">◆</span><span><strong>Primary CTA CTR</strong> ("Get Notion Free") segmented by hero variant — a direct signal of whether message clarity moves the needle.</span></div>
    <div class="bullet"><span class="dot">◆</span><span><strong>Scroll depth to the use-cases block</strong> — if the hero really is the weak point, attention should drop off before reaching the body, which is where engagement concentrates today.</span></div>
    <div class="bullet"><span class="dot">◆</span><span><strong>Hero bounce rate</strong> (custom event "hero_exit" vs. scroll) — to confirm whether the Clarity friction detected translates into real abandonment, not just a qualitative hypothesis.</span></div>
  </section>

  <footer>
    <p>This analysis was generated with <a href="https://auditor-cognitivo.vercel.app">Auditor Cognitivo</a>, a tool I built that combines CRO frameworks (LIFT Model) and cognitive psychology (Ekman, Zajonc, Theory of Mind) to audit ad copy, support messages, and landing pages.</p>
    <p class="tool-note">Santiago Batista Zelaya — Marketing, Operations, and Psychology (UBA) applied to conversion optimization.</p>
  </footer>

